Expected Outcomes
A working Maven project containing a Camel route with priority-based routing, validation, and transformation logic
A JUnit 5 test class using CamelTestSupport and mock endpoints that verifies all branches of the route logic, including two failure scenarios
The ability to read Maven Surefire console output and target/surefire-reports/ files to confirm whether tests passed or failed and why
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Mock endpoint never receives any messages, and assertMockEndpointsSatisfied() times out. Symptom: the test hangs for several seconds then fails with an AssertionError reporting Received: 0. Diagnostic hint: check that the string returned by isMockEndpoints() exactly matches the endpoint URIs used inside the route (including the direct: scheme), and confirm the message is actually being sent to the entry point of that route, not a different one.

Issue 2: throwException test fails because no exception was thrown, or the wrong message is asserted. Symptom: assertThrows does not fail immediately, but the assertion on ex.getCause().getMessage() throws a NullPointerException or reports a mismatched string. Diagnostic hint: remember that ProducerTemplate.sendBody() wraps route exceptions in a CamelExecutionException, so you must inspect ex.getCause().getMessage() rather than ex.getMessage() directly, and double check the exact XPath condition that should trigger the exception for your test's input data.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a small but realistic Camel application with priority-based routing, order validation, and message transformation, then wrote a complete JUnit 5 test suite for it using CamelTestSupport and mock endpoints. Along the way you learned how isMockEndpoints() transparently substitutes real endpoints with mocks during testing, how to set expectations with expectedMessageCount and expectedBodiesReceived, and how to read both the Maven console output and the target/surefire-reports/ files to confirm whether a test suite passed and to diagnose exactly which assertion failed when it did not. These are the core skills needed to safely refactor and extend Camel integration routes with confidence that existing behavior has not regressed. As a next step, consider exploring AdviceWithRouteBuilder for intercepting and modifying routes at test time without touching production code, and investigate Camel's camel-test-spring-junit5 module if your routes are wired together using Spring or Spring Boot.
